WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.320
Lipid bilayers are nearly impermeable to ions and most polar molecules, and the

2
00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.020
reason is energetic, not a simple matter of size.

3
00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:15.480
Crossing the hydrophobic core means shedding the shell of water an ion or polar

4
00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.860
group wears, and stripping that water off costs a lot of energy.

5
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:21.200
Hydrophobicity is the key variable.

6
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.400
Indole, the uncharged ring of tryptophan, slips through a membrane far more easily

7
00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:29.960
than tryptophan itself, even though the ring is nearly the same.

8
00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:31.740
Charge is the toll.

9
00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:36.660
Across the whole range, permeability spans about 12 orders of magnitude.

10
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:42.320
Membrane transport proteins sort into three families by speed and energy source.

11
00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:47.320
Channels are the fastest, up to about 10 million ions per second, but passive.

12
00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.660
They only let things move down their gradient, with selectivity built into their

13
00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:54.860
structure. Carriers are slower and can be regulated.

14
00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.560
They change shape to ferry their cargo across.

15
00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:03.180
Pumps are the slowest, around 100 cycles per second, but they spend ATP to push

16
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.600
molecules against their gradient.

17
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:10.100
Speed, regulation, and energy source together tell you which machine you are looking

18
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.100
at.

19
00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:16.490
The potassium channel moves ions at remarkable speed, probably by electrostatic

20
00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:22.330
repulsion. Several potassium ions sit in the selectivity filter at once, across four

21
00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:24.570
binding sites, S1 through S4.

22
00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:29.710
Because like charges repel, the arrival of one ion shoves the ion ahead of it out

23
00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:34.550
the far side, like balls knocking down an assembly line, or a Newton's cradle.

24
00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:40.450
That knock-on turns binding energy directly into exit energy, so the channel is

25
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:45.330
selective and fast at the same time, passing potassium at near the diffusion limit.

26
00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:51.390
Secondary active transport comes in two flavors, and both spend the sodium gradient

27
00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:57.430
the pump builds. Symporters move two species in the same direction, SGLT1 in the

28
00:01:57.430 --> 00:02:01.070
intestine drags glucose uphill by riding sodium downhill.

29
00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:06.830
Antiporters move them in opposite directions, the cardiac sodium-calcium exchanger

30
00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:10.130
pushes calcium out using the inward sodium flow.

31
00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:16.270
Neither one burns ATP directly; they borrow the energy stored in the sodium gradient

32
00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:19.290
like a waterwheel stealing energy from a current.

33
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:24.130
This sodium-driven kind ultimately depends on the sodium-potassium pump.

34
00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:29.000
Here is a practical decision tree for classifying any transport event.

35
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:33.780
First ask whether the molecule needs to cross at all, and whether it is small and

36
00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:39.300
nonpolar. If so, it simply diffuses, like oxygen into a red blood cell.

37
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.700
If it needs help, ask which direction it moves relative to its gradient.

38
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:48.000
Downhill through a protein is facilitated diffusion or a channel.

39
00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:54.640
Uphill means active transport, powered directly by ATP in a pump, or indirectly by

40
00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:57.480
an ion gradient in secondary active transport.

41
00:02:58.180 --> 00:02:59.840
Three questions sort every case.
